# spec-trio BACKLOG

Each line is one iteration's worth of work. `spec-trio.sh` selects the top unchecked line and runs planner → coder → tests → reviewer against it, marking it done only after success and any required merge — with `spec.md` as the load-bearing contract.

Format: GitHub-style task list (`- [ ] ...`). Cite the spec section the task satisfies so the planner has a starting hook (`§3.2`, `§5.1`, …). The planner is responsible for emitting `<allowed-paths>` based on what the spec actually permits.

## Tasks

- [ ] (replace this) implement §3.1 — `function_name(arg: type)` per the spec's stated semantics
- [ ] (replace this) add §5.1 coverage — a test that demonstrates §3.1's contract
- [ ] (replace this) wire §3.2 edge case — graceful degradation when `<condition>`

## Rules of the road

- Top of list = highest priority.
- Each task should map to one or more `§N` / `§N.M` spec sections. If a task doesn't fit the spec, the planner will emit `SPEC-MISMATCH` and stop — fix the task wording or amend `spec.md` (see "When to reach for debate" in the README) rather than fight the gate.
- The harness can append entries:
  - `NEEDS-FIX` and verification failures retry the same unchecked row; the driver passes failure-log context to the next attempt.
  - `--coverage-requeue` appends `- [ ] (spec coverage gap §5.N) <name>` for any test criterion that no commit cited.
  - Don't be surprised by new lines appearing mid-run.
- DISCUSS, UNKNOWN, OUT-OF-SCOPE or changed protected inputs stop the run for human attention. Never mark their tasks complete.
- This file is **state**. Commit it (or not) as you see fit, but be aware overnight runs will mutate it.
